  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nToday through Thu night. Upper level high pressure centered \nnear the four- corners region continues to shield all of \nsouthern Idaho from significant dry cold fronts. However, the \nsouth southwest air flow will continue to pump moist and \nunstable air into the Gem State. While it is not as unstable as \nthe previous weekend days, it will trigger isolated \nthunderstorms that should mainly be dry. There is little to no \nchange today through Thu afternoon, so temperatures will \nslightly warm as the moisture decreases and the upper level flow\nstarts to flatten and become more westerly. This is due to a \ncoastal trough that will break down the northern end of this \nupper level ridge starting Thu and continuing into the extended \nperiod. \n\nFri through Mon...As this trough swings through Thu night and\nFri, thunderstorms increase in coverage and in precipitation\namounts. The increased cloud cover means more mild overnight\nlows and cooler afternoon highs. By Sat night and Sun the trough\nhas moved far to the east. Thus a warming trend develops at this\npoint as skies clear. Wind stays breezy at least, and possibly\nwindy, which means just below Wind Advisory, and strong enough\nto reach critical fire weather thresholds. The big question is\nwhat will humidity do. At this time, it does not dry out enough.",
